{"parentUuid":"88c69628-ff80-4ad7-a6d8-c6cda1010cf5","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/home/dev/ledgerline","sessionId":"insight-10","version":"2.0.14","gitBranch":"fix/customer-csv-excel","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_01m7uoNjRakJ2lCDVTDiFJ8H","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The file itself is correct UTF-8: 'Müller' is stored as the two bytes c3 bc for ü. The problem is how Excel opens it. Excel on Windows does not detect UTF-8 in a CSV; unless the file starts with a byte order mark it reads it in the system's ANSI code page, Windows-1252 here. In Windows-1252 the bytes c3 bc are the two characters 'Ã¼', which is exactly what finance sees. Our file starts directly with 'id,name', with no BOM.\n\nPython can write the BOM for us with the encoding utf-8-sig, which puts the three bytes EF BB BF at the start. Excel then reads the file as UTF-8. Let me check who else reads this file first, since a BOM would show up as part of the first column name for a reader that opens it as plain utf-8."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","usage":{"input_tokens":747,"output_tokens":397}},"uuid":"10dbb422-55a1-4903-8437-043a84c8c1e6","timestamp":"2026-09-19T08:57:46.000Z","requestId":"req_011C9nIhkZhwJsbD8kLTUmPI"}
